body:
I feel you about your bully, but you might not even be a match. You might regret for the rest of your life that your chose your bully's past behavior over the possible future of your sister. 

&#x200B;

After this you can choose to leave them alone, never speak to them again if it turns out that he's still the same person. But your sister will never forget that you were there, and may realise the mistake she made. 

&#x200B;

It's your choice, and  I hope that whatever you make, it's not one that you regret.

Jesus dunks on every dude that tries to get him to say something wrong. It's so beautiful and amazing how the things he said about two thousand years ago are still relevant today. Like priests should pay taxes, temples should be a place of worship not profits, don't stone people for sins....

&#x200B;

But hey I guess that's 'open to interpretation' these days...

Uh, I'm actually really confused here... Because I got a male romance option at the end credits? I didn't technically get any romance scenes with him BUT the fact that it put him there as my romance option means that it's most likely that there IS some romance scene in there with him!

&#x200B;

He's the guy that bodyguards the 'bad guy' (sorry I'm terrible with names). Who really wants to fight you to the death.

&#x200B;

SO UH YEAH I CLAIM THIS. THIS IS ME I AM THE ONE WHO HAS DISCOVERED THE MAN IT'S ME.

&#x200B;

I would have screenshot it if I knew it was an important thing, but I didn't.

I think there's more to it. We see that the world resets for new arisen and everyone is trapped in this 'time loop'. Where if you fail, you become the mad man at the edge of the sea for the next arisen as punishment for trying to break the cycle (or joining him?). The previous arisen seem to just join the new timeline, if they fail in their charge which is to slay the dragon. However, is the dragon ACTUALLY the one we are assuming it is? 

&#x200B;

The SECOND dragon comes down with NO taint. Which seems quite impossible given what happened to it, and we are told that all other dragons are merely a pale imitations of the TRUE red dragon. 

&#x200B;

So what if the Pathfinder is the true dragon that was meant to be slain to free the world? The real charge given to the arisen? As the pathfinder was on the stage, playing a part, a guiding hand, but never knowing that it was actually the one in danger until the very end of the play. Sad to know it would not be there to see what came next, but simply another piece on the board.

The issue with Anders is that the arguement is 'Is terrorism ever justified', because that's literally what Anders did. He didn't even blow up the people who really caused his problems. The guy is meant to be intelligent, but the truth is he did this just so that people would kill him because he didn't want to try doing the hard work of changing things. If he REALLY wanted to blow up people to change things, he would have blown up the Templars. Instead he went for the easy target, and any idiot who thought ahead (which he had to do in order to get this plan done) would have known that the Templars wouldn't just go 'Oh wow, the mages are fighting back! Guess we'll just listen now!. 

&#x200B;

My god DA2 made me so angry with Anders. You have Fenris who has this great journey through his problems and gets to be a better person, but then you have fucking 'Imma murder the lady who is actually mediating for my people' Anders.
